---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I use a sauna during pregnancy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "ACOG advises that it is best not to use a sauna or hot tub early in pregnancy because overheating can be harmful. Pregnancy and health history vary, so ask your obstetric clinician or midwife for personal guidance rather than relying on a general temperature or time limit."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is an infrared sauna safer during pregnancy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A lower air temperature does not establish that an infrared sauna is safe during pregnancy. Both types expose the body to heat, and there is not enough evidence to recommend infrared sauna use as a safer alternative."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What should I do if I feel overheated during pregnancy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Leave the heat, move to a cooler place, and seek prompt medical guidance if symptoms such as fainting, confusion, chest pain, severe headache, persistent dizziness, or concerning pregnancy symptoms occur."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---
<Base
  title="Sauna During Pregnancy: Current Guidance and Safer Next Steps"
  description="Current pregnancy sauna guidance, why overheating matters, why infrared is not a proven safer option, and what to ask your clinician."
  canonical="/guides/sauna-during-pregnancy"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="text-sm text-amber-700 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Pregnancy safety</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3">Sauna During Pregnancy: Current Guidance and Safer Next Steps</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 mb-8">Source review &middot; August 2026</p>

    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-8 text-stone-700">
      <strong>Bottom line:</strong> The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists says it is best not to use a sauna or hot tub early in pregnancy because overheating can be harmful. Ask your obstetric clinician or midwife for advice specific to your pregnancy.
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-8">
      <img src="/images/lifestyle/sauna-interior-forest-view.jpg" alt="Empty wooden sauna interior" class="w-full object-cover max-h-80" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" />
    </div>

    <p class="text-lg text-stone-700 mb-6">Online advice often offers precise temperature limits or a number of "safe" minutes. Those rules can create false confidence because heat response differs by person, stage of pregnancy, sauna design, hydration, medication, and health history.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">What ACOG says</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">ACOG's patient guidance says it is best not to use a sauna or hot tub early in pregnancy. The concern is a rise in core body temperature. The same guidance notes that a hot tub can raise body temperature faster than a sauna because sweating is less effective in water.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-6">Primary source: <a class="text-amber-700 hover:underline" href="https://www.acog.org/womens-health/experts-and-stories/ask-acog/can-i-use-a-sauna-or-hot-tub-early-in-pregnancy" rel="noopener noreferrer">ACOG, "Can I use a sauna or hot tub early in pregnancy?"</a></p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why research does not create a DIY safe limit</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">Research on pregnancy and environmental heat is complex. A recent systematic review and meta-analysis examined maternal hyperthermia across exposures, but findings across controlled exercise, passive heat, climate, and observational studies cannot be reduced to one universal sauna rule.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">That is why this page does not recommend a session length, temperature, trimester exception, or postpartum return date. Those decisions belong with a clinician who knows the pregnancy and can account for individual risk.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-6">Research source: <a class="text-amber-700 hover:underline" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42127955/" rel="noopener noreferrer">systematic review and meta-analysis of maternal hyperthermia during pregnancy</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Infrared is not a proven safer workaround</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-700 mb-6">Infrared cabins often operate at lower air temperatures than traditional saunas, but lower air temperature alone does not prove lower pregnancy risk. Both expose the body to heat, products and protocols vary, and there is not enough evidence to recommend infrared use as a safer pregnancy alternative.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Questions to ask your clinician</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-stone-700 mb-8">
      <li>Should I avoid all sauna, hot-tub, steam-room, and high-heat exposure during my pregnancy?</li>
      <li>Do my medications, blood pressure, hydration, or pregnancy history change the risk?</li>
      <li>Which warning symptoms require urgent care?</li>
      <li>When can I resume sauna use after delivery, based on my recovery?</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-8">
      <h2 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">If you become overheated</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-700 text-sm">Leave the heat and move to a cooler environment. Seek prompt medical advice for fainting, confusion, chest pain, severe headache, persistent dizziness, or any pregnancy symptom that concerns you. In an emergency, use local emergency services.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">FAQ</h2>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Can I use a sauna during pregnancy?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">ACOG advises that it is best not to use a sauna or hot tub early in pregnancy. Ask your clinician for guidance specific to you.</p></div>
      <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Is an infrared sauna safer?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">It has not been established as a safer pregnancy option. Lower air temperature is not enough to determine risk.</p></div>
      <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Is there a safe number of minutes?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">This guide does not provide a universal limit. Individual heat response and pregnancy risk differ, so use clinician guidance.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6 mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">Related reading</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li><a href="/guides/sauna-health-benefits/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Sauna health evidence and limitations</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/how-long-to-stay-in-sauna/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">General sauna session safety</a></li>
        <li><a href="/how-we-review/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">How we review health and product claims</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
